    {
        "id": "gha-001",
        "title": "Secure Software Architecture: Design Before Controls",
        "concept": "secure-software-architecture",
        "category": "principle",
        "difficulty": 4,
        "media_id": "06f1eef0",
        "hook": "Secure software architecture is not a security add-on. It is the structural decision layer that determines how much security work your system will need forever after.",
        "description": "A deep lesson on how architectural boundaries, trust assumptions, and failure models shape the security posture of a system before code-level controls even appear.",
        "problem": "Teams often treat architecture as neutral plumbing, then discover too late that insecure trust boundaries, shared failure domains, and poorly separated privileges make every later control fragile and expensive.",
        "explanation": "Secure software architecture starts by deciding where trust should and should not exist: identity boundaries, data sensitivity boundaries, execution boundaries, and operational ownership boundaries. At expert level, the question is not only 'what control do we use' but 'what architecture makes control enforceable under load, partial failure, and operator error.' Good architecture reduces attack surface by isolating capabilities, constraining blast radius, separating duties, and making security-relevant behavior observable. In practice, that means explicit trust zones, least-privilege service interactions, secure defaults, resilient dependencies, and designs that remain understandable during incidents.",
        "summary": "Architecture is security's leverage point. If boundaries, privilege paths, and failure assumptions are wrong, later controls become patchwork."
    },
    {
        "id": "gha-002",
        "title": "Security by Design: Preventive Thinking at Architecture Time",
        "concept": "security-by-design",
        "category": "principle",
        "difficulty": 4,
        "media_id": "06f1eef0",
        "hook": "Security by design means the system is engineered so that common mistakes become harder to make and dangerous states become harder to reach.",
        "description": "An expert walkthrough of preventive design: secure defaults, reduction of implicit trust, and architectural guardrails that reduce both attacker opportunity and operator error.",
        "problem": "Organizations say security is important, but if the happy path is insecure and the secure path is operationally painful, real-world systems drift toward exposure.",
        "explanation": "Security by design is less about sprinkling controls everywhere and more about changing system incentives. Mature architectures bias toward safe behavior through default-deny access, opinionated platform baselines, short-lived credentials, protected secrets, and automated policy enforcement. The expert nuance is that preventive design must coexist with delivery speed, so the architecture should package security into paved roads rather than one-off exceptions. The strongest design removes whole classes of failure, not merely alerts on them after the fact.",
        "summary": "Design the platform so safe behavior is the default, dangerous behavior is exceptional, and recovery does not depend on heroics."
    },
